7. Frequently Asked Questions
Is a Local Lab a tutoring program?
No. Local Labs focus on mathematical investigation and discovery. They are not intended to provide routine homework help or tutoring.
Do I need to create my own math curriculum?
No. National Math Lab provides monthly investigation problems, solutions and resources. If local lab leaders want to teach math curriculum outside the investigation, they may request additional curriculum support when needed.
Does my Local Lab need to be hosted at a school?
No. Local Labs may operate in schools, libraries, community centers, or other suitable settings. Leaders are responsible for obtaining any necessary permission, but Math Lab can provide suggestions and tips and guide you along each step.
Does every Local Lab need to follow the same format?
No. Local Labs can adapt their session structure to their participants and setting while remaining aligned with Math Lab's mission. You can teach your own curriculum of your choice while also going over the monthly national investigation with your students.
Can I start a Local Lab if I do not already have a host location?
Yes. Identifying a suitable host location is an important part of establishing a Local Lab. Applicants can explore potential locations after applying.
Do I need to be an expert in mathematics?
No. Local Lab Leaders are responsible for organizing and facilitating opportunities for mathematical exploration, not for being experts in every mathematical topic.
How does my Local Lab contribute to Math Lab?
Each Local Lab expands access to mathematical investigation within its community. By connecting local activities to National Investigations, Local Labs help bring Math Lab's educational mission to more students.


